[00:01.84]Model Test 10
[00:04.41]Section A  
[00:05.71]Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations.
[00:11.60]At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions.
[00:15.92]Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once.
[00:20.37]After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D).
[00:29.57]Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the center.
[00:36.36]Conversation One
[00:39.27]W: Hey, you are packing up. 
[00:41.38]Where are you going? 
[00:43.00]What will you do with those things?
[00:45.33]M: I will throw them away. 
[00:47.09]I am moving to another apartment, and I don't need them anymore in the new room.
[00:52.15]W: I see. 
[00:53.12]Don't throw those electronic wastes carelessly! 
[00:56.39]They are dangerous for our environment! 
[00:59.10]We should protect our environment as best as possible, because it can influence our lives and our children's lives. 
[01:06.89]Besides, some damage we make is beyond repair.
[01:10.55]M: Really? What can I do with this broken cellphone and that old computer? 
[01:15.49]Should I keep all these useless things and fill up my new room with these things that are out-of-date?
[01:21.89]W: I don't say that. 
[01:23.68]But do you know that every year more than 10,000 computer monitors become obsolete in the US alone? 
[01:31.57]Electronic wastes are toxic and contaminate the soil if not recycled properly.
[01:37.90]M: I have never heard of that. 
[01:39.77]How do you know it?
[01:40.89]W: Look at this. 
[01:42.67]This battery contains poison, and this microchip contains arsenic and acid and other hazardous materials. 
[01:51.05]Those materials are not only contaminating the soil, they can also damage the brain and have bad effects on our health.
[01:59.77]M: Oh...I see. 
[02:01.35]So where should I throw these things then?
[02:04.27]W: You can drop your electronic wastes in the network box or sell them to be recycled.
[02:10.08]M: Can I sell my garbage for money?
[02:12.53]W: There are a lot of recycling companies which are willing to pay you a few bucks for your old stuff. 
[02:19.10]Just search on the Internet for that information.
[02:22.38]M: Alright, I think you are right. 
[02:24.60]Sorry for being rude.
[02:26.36]W: It's OK. 
[02:27.79]Why don't you join an environmental protection volunteering project with me? 
[02:32.69]We can do our bit to protect our environment.
[02:36.72]M: I'd love that!
[02:39.84]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[02:45.69]1. What is the man doing at the beginning of the conversation?
[03:04.33]2. What does the woman say about electronic wastes?
[03:22.54]3. What does the woman suggest the man do with his electronic wastes?
[03:41.86]4. What does the man probably do in the near future?
[04:00.22]Conversation Two
[04:03.79]W: Professor Wilson, do you think that children nowadays are naughtier than we used to be?
[04:09.75]M: Well, it's really depending. 
[04:11.72]Children in the big cities perhaps tend to be naughty.
[04:15.55]W: I agree with you. 
[04:18.05]I wonder why!
[04:20.32]M: Do you know that it's mostly caused by television?
[04:24.11]W: You can say that. 
[04:25.64]Television watching promotes violence, unsafe practices, and eating disorders in children. 
[04:31.48]According to a report, the average American child spends approximately 28 hours per week watching television. 
[04:39.25]Isn't that too long?
[04:41.04]M: Definitely. Imagine by the time a child reaches the age of 18, how many murders and other acts of violence will they have seen? 
[04:51.05]Oh, my God!
[04:52.98]W: To make things worse, American media is the most violent in the world; 80% of American television programs contain violence. 
[05:02.20]But I notice sometimes children who don't like watching TV are also naughty.
[05:07.17]M: They are influenced by our hostile environment. 
[05:11.32]Children are still unstable, and they are changeable.
[05:15.56]W: What is the best thing parents can do to stop violence on TV?
[05:20.61]M: I think it's rather hard to stop violence on TV, because children today have much more freedom and chance 
[05:27.13]to watch TV anywhere and anytime as well. 
[05:30.74]Moreover, the media often concern more about profit rather than quality.
[05:36.92]W: Sounds so disappointing.
[05:39.71]M: Parents must have a good communication with children and make them understand the harm of watching violence, 
[05:46.19]and children need to be controlled in watching TV.
[05:50.35]W: I agree with you. 
[05:51.66]But how can we at least eliminate bad TV programs, because I believe that parents can't control children all day long, right?
[06:00.87]M: Start from ourselves, don't watch uneducated programs; if possible, tell friends or relatives not to watch them either. 
[06:09.36]By doing so, hopefully the rating of the programs will be low, and in turn, TV stations will not broadcast low-rated programs.
[06:19.45]W: You're right. 
[06:20.88]Children look up to us as role models. 
[06:23.15]We should do our best to change the situation. 
[06:26.40]Thank you, Professor Wilson. 
[06:28.27]Glad to have you here.
[06:30.19]M: My pleasure.
[06:33.83]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[06:39.32]5. Why do children in the big cities tend to be naughty according to the man?
[06:58.84]6. How can watching television influence children according to the woman?
[07:18.03]7. What does the man say about the media?
[07:35.72]8. Why does the man advise parents not to watch uneducated programs?
[07:55.41]Section B 
[07:57.45]Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. 
[08:02.65]At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions.
[08:07.55]Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once.
[08:11.81]After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D).
[08:19.89]Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the center.
[08:27.21]Passage One
[08:29.17]Caffeine and napping have something in common.
[08:32.23]They both make you feel alert and can enhance your performance, whether that's driving, working or studying.
[08:39.83]But some people are convinced that drinking a cup of coffee before taking a nap gives you extra energy when you wake up.
[08:47.93]Drinking coffee can also help us get through the day. 
[08:52.07]And since the 1990s, researchers have been studying how combining the two might help.
[08:59.15]In a 1997 study, 12 sleep-deprived people drank the equivalent of one large cup of brewed coffee and five minutes later had the chance to nap for 15 minutes. 
[09:11.74]Then they did some driving tests in a simulator to check their alertness.
[09:16.34]Although drinking a coffee (without a nap) helped their driving performance, combining caffeine with a nap¡ª
[09:23.10]a coffee nap¡ªimproved it even further.
[09:26.41]People who took a coffee nap were less likely to drift out of their lanes on a two-hour monotonous simulated drive.
[09:33.94]This was compared to when they just drank a cup of coffee (and had no nap) or when they had a cup of decaffeinated coffee (and without a nap).
[09:44.06]A coffee nap even helped performance if people dozed during their nap time rather than falling into a deeper sleep. 
[09:51.57]A coffee nap also reduced sleepiness once people got up, with people remaining alert for a couple of hours.
[09:59.40]However, this early, small study was questioned. 
[10:03.47]For instance, we don't know how much coffee the people in the study were used to drinking 
[10:08.73]or if they were what researchers call caffeine-naive and so more likely to experience a greater caffeine ¡°hit.¡±
[10:16.37]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[10:23.24]9. What did the 1997 study find about people who took a coffee nap?
[10:43.95]10. What did the study find about the benefit of a coffee nap?
[11:03.36]11. Why was the study questioned according to the speaker?
[11:22.15]Passage Two
[11:24.28]With its world-famous Opera House, Sydney is easily Australia's most recognizable city. 
[11:32.89]It's also a hot property market. 
[11:36.29]The country's low interest and mortgage rates have driven sales of affordable middle income housing in recent years. 
[11:45.97]But with demand so high, there is barely enough stock to satisfy hungry homebuyers.
[11:54.20]Now one company believes they have the solution that will both save space and keep city living cost effective¡ªshipping containers. 
[12:06.80]From its base in the town of Lismore, The Container Build Group is transforming the durable metal boxes into comfortable homes 
[12:17.67]that will eventually be transported to locations across Australia. 
[12:22.73]According to the company's CEO, the concept provides a clean and productive way to provide affordable urban housing.
[12:32.97]So far, the business is quite profitable. 
[12:36.81]Annual turnover last year was over $1.5 million and the company expects to double that going forward. 
[12:46.80]There are environmental and construction advantages as well. 
[12:51.55]The ecological advantages of building with a container is the recycling of a container. 
[12:58.97]The company tries to use as many eco-friendly products as possible. 
[13:04.76]There's virtually no on-site work to build it and a house can be built from scratch in as little as three weeks. 
[13:14.34]Clients can also personalize the design to make their homes truly unique.
[13:21.42]Many still doubt whether people will want to live in recycled steel boxes, but with more than 34 million shipping containers 
[13:32.07]either in use or shipping around the world, an increasing number of architects are aware of the possibilities they provide.
[13:43.50]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[13:49.09]12. What problem is Sydney's property market facing in recent years?
[14:09.20]13. What does the company's CEO say about transforming shipping containers into homes?
[14:31.13]14. What can we learn about the company's business of transforming shipping containers into homes?
[14:52.75]15. What makes container houses eco-friendly?
[15:11.23]Section C 
[15:13.26]Directions: In this section, you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. 
[15:22.55]The recordings will be played only once.
[15:25.29]After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D).
[15:33.58]Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the center.
[15:40.63]Recording One
[15:42.99]Welcome to my lecture! 
[15:44.99]Today, I will tell you a maxim¡ªgiving up. 
[15:48.27]When you try too hard but you don't succeed; when despite all your efforts you don't get the results you need, give up and move on. 
[15:57.42]Drop it like it's hot, and run in the opposite direction as fast as you can.
[16:02.28]Confused? 
[16:03.89]What is wrong with giving up?
[16:06.09]Oh, yes, we all have been trained and conditioned to work hard to achieve our dreams, and that is a good thing.
[16:14.06]The problem arises when all these inspirational quotes start confusing us. 
[16:19.03]These messages tell people not to give up, but they don't tell you that it should only apply to your true dreams. 
[16:26.42]They tell one to ¡°try and try again¡± until they succeed, but that amount of effort should only be put 
[16:32.97]towards what you really want in life and one should never quit what they think to be their life's purpose. 
[16:39.63]The rest is all just there to confuse you, distract you, give you a headache, heartache and depress you, so don't let it.
[16:48.27]If you suck at piano and you know it's because you don't even like the instrument, give it up.
[16:54.56]If no matter how hard you study you can hardly score an above average grade, don't worry, your talent lies somewhere else. 
[17:03.04]Not everybody is an A student.
[17:05.63]If you always end up burning the chocolate cake you are trying to bake, maybe it is because you were born to buy one and not make one. 
[17:13.70]Quit baking.
[17:15.55]Are you the worst performer on your sales team, and even the interns are better than you are? 
[17:21.18]It's because you were not supposed to be a sales person, you were meant to be a DJ.
[17:26.38]Try to recognize your talent, your ability and your skills. 
[17:30.95]Find out what you are good at, what makes you happy and then work on it. 
[17:36.23]If you do, no one would have to tell you or inspire you to give up because when you love something, 
[17:42.62]you keep trying until you get it right and the enthusiasm flows from within.
[17:48.31]Don't worry about what people will say or think. 
[17:51.05]It's your life. 
[17:52.65]You have to live it and you have to be your own boss.
[17:56.60]If at the end of the day, you have a sense of fulfillment, peace of mind, a smile on your face and can sleep well at night, 
[18:04.63]you can be sure you are making the right decisions. 
[18:08.32]Even if some of them are deciding to give up on certain things in your life.
[18:14.46]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[18:19.97]16. What can we learn about the speaker's attitude towards giving up?
[18:39.60]17. Under what circumstances should people NOT give up?
[18:59.18]18. What do the examples about failure illustrate?
[19:18.55]Recording Two
[19:19.99]Hello, everyone. 
[19:21.25]In today's lecture, we will say something about a folk musician. 
[19:25.83]The name of this special musician is Jamey Turner. 
[19:30.03]Turner often performs to large crowds in Old Town Alexandria, Virginia, a short drive from Washington, D.C. 
[19:38.56]You can find Turner playing an unusual musical instrument, the glass harp, near Alexandria's historic waterfront. 
[19:47.12]A glass harp is made up of different-sized stemmed drinking glasses, each filled with water. 
[19:54.23]Turner makes music by running his fingers over the tops of the glasses. 
[19:59.03]By doing so, each glass makes a different musical sound. 
[20:03.76]Visitors to Alexandria like what they see and hear. 
[20:07.71]They thank Turner by leaving a dollar or more in his box for donations.
[20:13.06]Nicole Schwarss is a visitor from Germany. 
[20:16.99]¡°I think it's fantastic. 
[20:18.89]I've never heard anything like this before,¡± she said. 
[20:22.97]Jamey Turner is 78 years old. 
[20:26.13]He says he has been playing the glass harp for 50 years. 
[20:31.15]Turner says he became interested at the age of six, when he heard his father playing with a glass of water at the dinner table. 
[20:39.51]Today Turner puts together his instrument by placing 60 different glasses on a wooden soundboard. 
[20:46.69]He holds the glasses in place with rubber bands, which keeps them from breaking. 
[20:51.93]He then adds different amounts of water to each one to create different musical notes. 
[20:57.81]Turner says adding or taking away just a little water will change the sound. 
[21:02.83]Adding water to a glass will make a lower sound. 
[21:06.73]The smaller bowls of water produce a higher sound, while the larger ones produce a lower, deeper sound. 
[21:14.59]Turner says he uses low-priced glass because it sounds better than costly crystal. 
[21:20.85]And he has more control over the sound. 
[21:23.91]Before buying new glasses, he will test them in stores. 
[21:28.45]He decides if they produce the right sound by rubbing his finger over the top of them.
[21:33.59]The glass harp was popular 300 years ago when composers like Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart wrote music for the instrument. 
[21:43.69]There have been about 400 musical compositions written just for the glass harp. 
[21:49.11]But Turner plays all kinds of music, even popular music from China. 
[21:54.81]Turner says he has performed all over the United States and in Japan. 
[22:00.06]Few people play the glass harp these days, but Turner says he sees videos of people experimenting with the instrument on YouTube. 
[22:09.15]He hopes the next generation will continue making its music.
[22:15.96]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[22:22.04]19. How did Turner become interested in playing the glass harp?
[22:41.58]20. What kind of glasses does Turner play?
[22:59.52]21. What music does Turner play?
[23:17.73]Recording Three
[23:19.71]It's time to move over. 
[23:21.75]A new study has found that sitting next to your boss may have a negative effect on the quality of your work.
[23:28.62]Researchers in the Netherlands found that physical distance is a key factor in whether or not the bad behavior of bosses spreads to their employees.
[23:38.08]In a series of studies, the researchers found that participants were more likely to treat others unfairly and act unethically 
[23:46.16]when in the close proximity to their bosses.
[23:49.08]Scientists at the Rotterdam School of Management, led by Gijs Van Houwelingen, created five experimental simulations 
[23:58.08]to test the relationship between physical distance and office conduct.
[24:02.86]¡°If someone kicks a dog right in front of you, it'll make you very mad,¡± Van Houwelingen told Co. Design. 
[24:10.69]¡°But if you hear about someone somewhere in the world kicking a dog, you probably won't feel as mad about it.¡±
[24:17.95]Distance can cause a person to become emotionally detached from a particular situation.
[24:23.92]The team created a simulation in which 150 undergrad business students played the role of middle manager 
[24:31.41]with two subordinate employees and a boss, according to Co. Design. 
[24:37.07]In this simulation, the participants were told different locations for their bosses' whereabouts, either in the same room, or across campus. 
[24:46.35]The researchers then gave assignments to the participants, and sent them ¡°fair¡± and ¡°unfair¡± messages, 
[24:53.41]to either explain the decision reasonably or make it appear as though the boss was just passing down unwanted work.
[25:00.94]The participants were then asked their opinions on the boss' behavior, and told to decide how they would treat their own employees.
[25:09.70]Participants who were physically near, or personally identified with the boss, were more likely to exhibit the same bad behavior.
[25:18.56]¡°We saw that the more distant someone is, they're less likely to identify with their boss or describe themselves in relation to their boss,¡± Van Houwelingen says.
[25:30.10]Spatial distance proved to be a major factor, but the research suggests there are other contributors that determine ethical behavior. 
[25:38.96]People who feel the need to belong to a social group will be more likely to copy the conduct of others. 
[25:46.47]Van Houwelingen suggests that offices find a balance that allows for physical distance along with collaboration and strong relationships between employees.
[25:57.38]¡°With distance, you get detached from what happens around you,¡± Van Houwelingen says. 
[26:03.91]¡°You might be more likely to evaluate behavior on its own merits.¡±
[26:11.04]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[26:17.44]22. What can we learn from the finding of the new study?
[26:36.36]23. What will probably happen if one sits too close to his boss?
[26:56.48]24. What does the example about kicking a dog show?
[27:15.85]25. What contributes to determining ethical behavior according to the speaker?